From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pixel5 <pixel5@localhost>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 23:40:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] LOCAL: Input: s6sy761 - add an SPI transport

The Pixel 5 touch controller, a Samsung S6SY79x, sits on SPI (QUP0 SE0)
and speaks the same command set and event format as the S6SY761: sense
on 0x10, events 0x60/0x61, boot status 0x55, panel info 0x23, power
mode 0xe4. Only the bus framing differs. Google's downstream driver
(redbull kernel, drivers/input/touchscreen/sec_touch/sec_ts.c, Samsung,
GPL-2.0) wraps every register access in a sync byte, 16-bit lengths
and a byte-sum checksum, padded to 4 bytes; a read is a request
transfer, a short delay, then a separate read of the reply.

This moves the driver's bus access behind two callbacks (read and
write a register), keeps the I2C path byte-for-byte equivalent, and
adds an SPI driver with that framing. Also: an optional reset GPIO
(pulsed at power-on, as downstream does) and polling for the
boot-complete event (up to 1 s) instead of a single read after 140 ms.
---
 drivers/input/touchscreen/Kconfig             |   5 +-
 drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c           | 496 +++++++++---
 ...L-Input-s6sy761-add-an-SPI-transport.patch | 755 ++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 1161 insertions(+), 95 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 out/0001-LOCAL-Input-s6sy761-add-an-SPI-transport.patch

